Errata applied automatically
============================

A LX2160A / LX2162A board can include one file and it gets every
work-around its silicon revision needs:

    lx2160a_auto_errata.rcwi        LX2160A rev 1
    lx2160a_rev2_auto_errata.rcwi   LX2160A rev 2
    lx2162a_auto_errata.rcwi        LX2162A

It replaces the #include of lx2160a.rcwi, so the board is not listing
errata one #include at a time any more.

Why
---

A board that names its errata by hand is only as complete as the day it
was written. Boards live downstream for years: a work-around added
upstream afterwards never reaches them and nothing says so.

Using it
--------

Declare the SerDes protocols as macros, then include the catalog. This
is lx2160ardb_rev2/XGGFF_PP_HHHH_RR_19_5_2/rcw_2000_700_2900_19_5_2.rcw
in full:

    /*
     * SerDes Protocol 1 -  19
     * SerDes Protocol 2 -  5
     * SerDes Protocol 3 -  2
     * ...
     */

    #define SRDS_PRTCL_S1_VAL 19
    #define SRDS_PRTCL_S2_VAL 5
    #define SRDS_PRTCL_S3_VAL 2

    #include <../lx2160asi/lx2160a_rev2_auto_errata.rcwi>

    SYS_PLL_RAT=14
    ...
    SRDS_PLL_REF_CLK_SEL_S1=2
    ...

    /* the board's own PBI, unchanged */
    #include <../lx2160asi/scratchrw1.rcw>
    #include <../lx2160asi/bootlocptr_nor.rcw>
    #include <../lx2160asi/common.rcw>
    #include <../lx2160asi/flexspi_divisor_28.rcw>

The catalog assigns SRDS_PRTCL_S1, S2 and S3 from those macros, so a
board states each protocol once and does not write the assignments
itself. A SerDes the board leaves powered down is declared as protocol
0. The LX2162A has no SerDes 3: SRDS_PRTCL_S3_VAL is not declared, and
lx2162a_auto_errata.rcwi marks the controllers its missing lanes would
have fed, PCIe2, PCIe5 and PCIe6, as absent whatever the protocols say.

The include belongs at the top of the board file, where lx2160a.rcwi
used to be, so the errata are now the first PBI the Service Processor
executes rather than the last.

What a board gets
-----------------

Only the writes its protocols justify. serdes_prtcl.rcw maps each
protocol of RM tables 289, 290 and 291 to the PCI Express, SATA and
Ethernet blocks it enables, and each erratum keeps the writes aimed at
an enabled block. On the board above, protocols 19 / 5 / 2 put PCI
Express lanes on PCIe3 and PCIe5 only and no XFI lane on SerDes 2, so
a050479 and the a050426 scrub drop the other four controllers and the
SerDes 2 XFI RAM, and the PBL goes from 3764 to 3156 bytes. Boards with
fewer interfaces save more:

    lx2160aqds_rev2/PPPP_PPPP_PPPP_PPPP_PPPP_PPPP_RR_1_2_2   3812 -> 1112
    lx2162aqds/SSSS_NNNN_PPPP_PPPP_RR_2_2                    3716 ->  672

Over the LX2160A and LX2162A directories the images shrink by a third,
from 306288 to 204528 bytes.

A group of awrites is never emitted without the loadacwindow it is
relative to: the window is loaded as soon as one group that needs it is
kept, see a050426.rcw.

A board that declares no protocol is still correct: every erratum is
emitted at full size, exactly as before. The declaration is opt-in and
no existing board changes behaviour by accident.

The catalog also completes the boards whose hand-written list fell
short of their siblings; the commit log says which ones.

Checking it
-----------

    ./check_errata_trim.sh [reference-rev]      (default: origin/devel)

builds the tree three times: the reference revision, the current tree
with RCW_ERRATA_NO_TRIM defined (every conditional block enabled) and
the current tree normally, and asserts

    reference <= no-trim    nothing the reference emitted is lost
    trimmed   <= no-trim    trimming never invents a command
    same window             every awrite the trimmed build keeps runs
                            under the loadacwindow it ran under in the
                            no-trim build

What the no-trim build gains on top of the reference, and what the
trimmed build adds to a board compared with the reference, is reported
rather than failed: that is the catalog completing a board.
